<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        Theme-invariant semantic tokens: spacing, typography, radius.

        Colour tokens are NOT here — they differ per theme and live in
        Themes/*.xaml, one complete set each. ADR-011: "a missing dark value is
        a bug, not a fallback."

        Only what a surface consumes is defined. ADR-011 rule 4: "New tokens
        are added when a surface needs one — not speculatively." Slice 1's
        interaction foundation added the icon, compact control, selection
        indicator and inner focus tokens below, each with a consumer in the
        same change. The settings surface added Heading type, the 24 spacing
        step, the Medium radius and the group padding; BodyStrong is Body at
        the emphasis weight, so it needs no size of its own. Motion arrives
        with the motion step (below). The remaining groups (Display/Code,
        Card/Floating/Overlay/Dialog, semantic colour, elevation, note1…note6)
        have no consumer and are deliberately absent.
    -->

    <!--
        SPACING — ADR-011 fixes this scale: 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48,
        "no value outside it". The values are the ADR's, not a choice made here.
        Only the four the folder pane needs are declared.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="SpaceXS">4</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="SpaceS">8</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="SpaceM">12</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="SpaceL">16</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="SpaceXL">24</x:Double>

    <!--
        The padding of a list row, named for the role rather than for one
        component. A note row is a list row, so it takes this rather than
        borrowing FolderRowPadding — the same reason the text inputs stopped
        borrowing it.

        It deliberately carries the same value as FolderRowPadding: the two
        lists replace each other on screen, and a note list denser than the
        folder list it replaced would read as a glitch. That agreement is a
        convention held by these two values being equal, NOT a guarantee —
        they are separate tokens, so changing one does not change the other.
        Anyone retuning list density has to change both on purpose.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="ListRowPadding">12,8,12,8</Thickness>

    <Thickness x:Key="FolderRowPadding">12,8,12,8</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PanePadding">8</Thickness>

    <!--
        A note's card (the visual refresh): a step more room inside than a list row, so a note reads as a page rather
        than a line, and one step of space between cards, so each card's edge is what separates it from the next. The
        editor fills the same padding when the card is edited, so the text does not move when editing starts.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="CardPadding">16,12,16,12</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="CardSpacing">0,0,0,8</Thickness>

    <!--
        Text inputs carry their own padding rather than borrowing the folder
        row's. The two happen to share values today, but they answer different
        questions — how dense the list is, versus how much room a caret needs —
        and a token named for a row cannot be retuned for one without silently
        changing the other (ADR-011: tokens are named by role).
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="InputPadding">12,8,12,8</Thickness>

    <!--
        The inside of a group of settings: one step of the scale on every side,
        so a group's rows line up with each other and away from its border.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="GroupPadding">12</Thickness>

    <!--
        Room for a scroll bar beside scrolled content, so the bar is never drawn over it. WinUI's scroll bar overlays
        its viewer's content and widens to ScrollBarSize (16) when the pointer reaches it.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="ScrollBarGutter">0,0,16,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        A settings switch as wide as its track as drawn. WinUI's switch template lays the track out from its left and
        keeps the On/Off text's column after it: measured (UI Automation, Settings page), a switch that sized itself
        stopped 86px short of the row's right edge. As wide as the track, it is flush with the other controls. The track
        is drawn 44 wide (measured in pixels, Invoke-Visual.ps1 case 6): at 40, the element's box was flush but the
        template clipped the track's rounded end. Not a step of the scale: the template's.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="SettingSwitchWidth">44</x:Double>

    <!--
        The narrowest a setting's choice (a ComboBox) is: its longest item today, "Use Windows setting", measured 174
        wide with its chevron (Invoke-Settings case 15), and a little room. See SettingComboBoxStyle for why it is fixed.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="SettingChoiceMinWidth">180</x:Double>

    <!--
        The widest a column of reading text grows (the welcome, ADR-021): about 60 characters of Body type. In the default
        360 DIP drawer it is never reached; in a drawer widened past it, the column stays this wide and centred, so a
        line stays short enough to read. Not a step of the scale: a measure of text, not of space.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="ReadingWidth">440</x:Double>

    <!--
        The gap between an icon and its label, everywhere one leads a label: a folder row, the Recycle Bin button. One
        value, so every icon-and-label pair in the pane lines up the same way.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="IconLabelSpacing">12</x:Double>

    <!--
        TYPOGRAPHY — Body, Caption and Heading.

        14px body matches the Windows shell default, so Noto reads as a native
        application rather than a web page; 12px caption is the smallest size
        that stays legible at 100% scaling. Line heights are 20 and 16 — ~1.4x,
        loose enough for a dense list to remain scannable.

        SemiBold (600) is the emphasis weight: headings and section names. A selected row keeps its weight (the visual
        refresh): the pill and the fill show selection, and text that changed weight reflowed the row as it moved.
    -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilyBody">Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>

    <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeBody">14</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightBody">20</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeCaption">12</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightCaption">16</x:Double>

    <!--
        Heading: 20/28, Windows 11's subtitle size, for a page title (the
        settings surface). One step above body is enough in a 360 DIP column.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeHeading">20</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="LineHeightHeading">28</x:Double>

    <!--
        The editor's type (ADR-017): headings 1-3 step down from the heading size to just above body; levels 4-6
        share level 3's. Code in the system's monospace, which Windows 11 ships; Consolas where it does not.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeHeading2">17</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeHeading3">15</x:Double>
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilyCode">Cascadia Mono, Consolas</FontFamily>

    <!--
        A card's checkboxes (☐ ☑) in the system's monochrome symbol font: left to the body font, ☑ falls back to the
        colour emoji font and draws in its own purple (measured, the visual refresh), not the text's colour.
    -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamilySymbol">Segoe UI Symbol</FontFamily>

    <FontWeight x:Key="FontWeightRegular">Normal</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="FontWeightEmphasis">SemiBold</FontWeight>

    <!--
        RADIUS — ADR-011 names four (Small, Medium, Large, Pill); only the two
        a surface uses are declared.
    -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusSmall">4</CornerRadius>
    <!--
        A group of settings, a note's card, and the controls inside a settings group: the owner chose one radius for a
        group and its controls (2026-11-06, #144), as a step down read as a sharp corner inside the rounder group.
    -->

    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusMedium">8</CornerRadius>

    <!--
        BORDER WIDTHS — the focus ring is 2px because a 1px ring is easy to
        lose against a selected background, and "focus is mandatory" (ADR-011).
        Inside it, a 1px inner stroke of the opposite tone: the two-tone ring
        Windows 11 draws, visible on any fill, the accent included.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderThicknessSubtle">1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderThicknessFocus">2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderThicknessFocusInner">1</Thickness>

    <!--
        ICONS AND COMPACT CONTROLS — a 16px glyph in a 32px square: the size
        Windows 11 uses for its own icon buttons, and a pointer target large
        enough at 100% scaling. The glyph font is WinUI's own symbol font
        (Segoe Fluent Icons, Segoe MDL2 Assets on Windows 10), not a token.
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="IconSize">16</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="ControlHeightCompact">32</x:Double>

    <!--
        SELECTION INDICATOR — the pill on the leading edge of a selected row,
        3 by 16 with rounded ends, as Windows' own lists draw it. Selection is
        then shown by the pill, the fill AND the weight, so it never depends on
        colour alone, and in high contrast, where hover and selection share the
        Highlight fill, the pill is what tells them apart.
    -->
    <!--
        MOTION (Slice 1.3, F2) — the drawer moves, never the window (ADR-011).
        Each role NAMES the WinUI resource that holds its value, so Noto never
        copies WinUI's numbers: Fast is ControlFastAnimationDuration (167 ms),
        Faster is ControlFasterAnimationDuration (83 ms), the curve is
        ControlFastOutSlowInKeySpline (0,0,0,1): fast, then settling. They are
        read by key at run time from the application's resources, which hold
        WinUI's own dictionary; a key that is missing turns motion off rather
        than guessing a value.

        The drawer slides in over Fast and out over Faster: closing is the same
        motion, quicker. It travels its own width, so it has no distance token.
        A surface entering travels Noto's distance, in DIPs. Normal (250 ms) has
        no consumer and is absent.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="MotionDrawerEnterDuration">ControlFastAnimationDuration</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="MotionDrawerExitDuration">ControlFasterAnimationDuration</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="MotionSurfaceDuration">ControlFastAnimationDuration</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="MotionEasing">ControlFastOutSlowInKeySpline</x:String>
    <x:Double x:Key="MotionSurfaceDistance">16</x:Double>

    <!-- Inset from the row's edge, inside the focus ring: a selected row that has focus still shows its pill (measured,
         the visual refresh: at the edge, the ring's 2px stroke covered the 3px pill). -->
    <Thickness x:Key="SelectionIndicatorMargin">4,0,0,0</Thickness>
    <x:Double x:Key="SelectionIndicatorWidth">3</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="SelectionIndicatorHeight">16</x:Double>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusIndicator">1.5</CornerRadius>

    <!-- A coloured card's marker (F7, #150): a bar along its leading edge, inside the rounded corners. -->
    <x:Double x:Key="NoteColorMarkerWidth">4</x:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="NoteColorMarkerMargin">4,8,0,8</Thickness>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusNoteColorMarker">2</CornerRadius>

</ResourceDictionary>
